  "finding": {
    "envelope": "examples/cycle_counting_power.py sweeps how many of the 3000 selected genes carry the ring. Across the time-axis designs the count only succeeds when essentially all of them do, and at the measured diurnal r ~ 0.70 the simulated gap is 1.2-1.4 - the failing clouds sit exactly on that curve. This is a real limitation of the criterion and it is measured, not assumed.",
    "headline": "On measured data the counting criterion returns the design's own number for 2 of 5 designs: the yearlong series and the yeast cell cycle. The cell cycle is the one design whose cyclic parameter is not a clock -- every other design here turns on a time axis -- and it is the design that separates counting axes from counting cycles: three recorded axes, one cycle, and the count is one.",
    "what_fails_and_why": "diurnal (GSE93066), forced desync (GSE48113) and the pooled annual x diurnal design all have a smoothly decaying H1 spectrum (gap 1.06-1.36), so there is no dominant bar and the count is 0. The cycles are nevertheless present: the circular Mantel correlation against the design's own axis is +0.70 for the diurnal cloud, i.e. the same strength as the yearlong series that *is* counted. The limitation is the power of the dominant-bar test, not the biology.",
    "what_works": "psme_annual: 5 Douglas-fir trees x 16 dates over a full year. Replicate-centred, the H1 spectrum has a dominant bar (gap ~4.9) and the count is 1, as the design predicts. The window sweep is the same statement from the other side: any sub-year window is an arc and counts 0. cellcycle_two_genotypes (GSE8799): 2 genotypes x 2 elutriations x 30 time points at 8-min resolution, i.e. *three* recorded design axes (genotype, replicate, cell-cycle time) and *one* cyclic parameter. Replicate-centred the dominant-bar gap is 3.6 and the count is 1. The two other recorded axes carry no cycle: genotype is a strong two-valued shift (Mantel r = +0.46, p <= 0.0005 on the uncentred cloud) and the elutriation is an offset, so the count is one and not three -- and without removing those two axis means the same cloud returns zero. The phase alignment is significant but modest: circular Mantel r = +0.16, p <= 0.0005, at the 110-130 min period the periodogram gives.",
    "where_the_envelope_does_not_explain_everything": "The power sweep explains the diurnal and forced-desync failures but not the yeast success: there the circular Mantel r is only +0.16, far below the +0.70 the failing diurnal cloud has, and yet the H1 spectrum still shows a dominant bar. A large gap and a large r are therefore two different kinds of evidence - the ring is present geometrically even where the design axis explains little of the distance variance - and it is the first kind that the count needs. The same asymmetry is what the identifiability rule in examples/cycle_identifiability.py is built to expose.",
    "why_the_forced_desync_cloud_cannot_test_the_claim": "human_forced_desync has circular Mantel r = +0.045 against the circadian axis: the circadian programme is not in the 3000 highest-variance genes of whole blood at all, so no method restricted to those genes could count that cycle.",
    "why_the_single_genotypes_behave_differently": "Split by genotype, the two 30-sample clouds do not behave alike. The wild type alone has no dominant bar (gap 1.35) and counts 0. The cyclin mutant alone has a dominant bar (gap 5.15) and counts 1 - but its circular Mantel correlation against the cell-cycle phase is only +0.06, so the bar that dominates is not the one the design names, and the decision rule would call that cloud 'inconsistent: check the design before reporting'. The pooled 60-sample cloud is the one whose dominant bar does track the phase (r = +0.16, p = 0.0005), which is why the headline count is quoted on it. A large gap, in other words, is not by itself evidence that the integer is the design's - the same caveat the identifiability rule exists to enforce."
  },
